From: Oever Gonzalez <software@notengobattery.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7996: quiesce the irq path before NAPI teardown

mt7996_unregister_device() disables the irq tasklet only after
mt7996_dma_cleanup() has already run napi_disable()/netif_napi_del()
on every queue. The tasklet's whole job is napi_schedule(), so an
interrupt pending in that window puts a deleted NAPI back on the
per-CPU softirq poll list; once the module is freed, net_rx_action()
walks the stale entry and jumps through a NULL ->poll. Observed in
production on a W1700K whose wedged WFDMA held its RX interrupt
asserted: rmmod warned six times in page_pool_disable_direct_recycling,
and the subsequent modprobe died with "unable to handle kernel execute
from non-executable memory at 0" in __napi_poll (execute-from-NULL,
panic in interrupt).

Mask both hifs' interrupts and disable the tasklet before tearing the
DMA down. This also keeps the tasklet's MT_INT_MCU_CMD branch from
launching mt7996_reset() in the middle of unregister if a dying chip
raises an MCU error there.

--- a/mt7996/init.c
+++ b/mt7996/init.c
@@ -1838,11 +1838,15 @@ void mt7996_unregister_device(struct mt7996_dev *dev)
 	mt7996_wed_rro_free(dev);
 	mt7996_mcu_exit(dev);
 	mt7996_tx_token_put(dev);
+	/* a pending irq would napi_schedule() a NAPI dma_cleanup is deleting */
+	mt76_wr(dev, MT_INT_MASK_CSR, 0);
+	if (dev->hif2)
+		mt76_wr(dev, MT_INT1_MASK_CSR, 0);
+	tasklet_disable(&dev->mt76.irq_tasklet);
 	mt7996_dma_cleanup(dev);
 	if (mt7996_has_hwrro(dev) &&
 	    !mtk_wed_device_active(&dev->mt76.mmio.wed))
 		mt7996_rro_msdu_page_map_free(dev);
-	tasklet_disable(&dev->mt76.irq_tasklet);

 	mt76_free_device(&dev->mt76);
 }
